{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense fails because it conflates 'selecting a theory for further testing' with 'preferring a theory for practical action.' The problem explicitly notes that scientists (and Popper) prefer better-corroborated theories when 'choosing which theory to act on.' Acting on a theory (e.g., building a bridge based on it) inherently relies on the expectation of its future reliability/success, not merely its utility as a target for falsification. By re-framing preference solely as a methodological rule for selecting falsification targets, the target ignores the 'action' component of the prompt, thereby failing to address whether the preference for action smuggles in induction.","premise":"The problem distinguishes between surviving tests and 'choosing which theory to act on,' implying that action requires an expectation of future success.","successor_question":"If corroboration offers no reason to believe a theory will succeed in future applications, on what non-inductive basis does Popper justify acting on the better-corroborated theory rather than the less-corroborated one?","target":"1ddb259890880286201f3a8df51e325138d9168d6972226ed48d300872dd7ffa"}]}